00:03:11.940 --> 00:03:15.000
so latent diffusion models,&nbsp;
was published on ArXiv.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.880
Okay, so, this timeline is just so we can better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:21.300
imagine how crowded the idea&nbsp;
space of diffusion models is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:29.100
Now we can sit back and relax from the diffusion&nbsp;
model hype, right? No, of course we can’t!

00:03:29.100 --> 00:03:34.920
We just got used to models creating images&nbsp;
from text, but now we quickly move onto the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:40.260
space of videos created from just a text&nbsp;
description! Video diffusion models have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:46.620
already been a thing since April 2022 with very&nbsp;
short, GIF-like animations of still images.

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:53.220
The big splash in video diffusion was caused&nbsp;
by MetaAI that has shown through a paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.220 --> 00:04:00.120
they haven’t released the model (yet?)… So&nbsp;
MetaAI showcases Make-A-Video, a diffusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:06.600
model generating impressive videos from text&nbsp;
descriptions on the 29th of September 2022.

00:04:07.740 --> 00:04:14.400
Then only a few days later, October the 5th,&nbsp;
Google Research announces its own artillery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:20.400
video diffusion models with Imagen Video, a&nbsp;
direct competitor to MetaAI’s Make-A-Video,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:26.820
producing short videos from text with a realism&nbsp;
we have not yet seen before, and with Phenaki,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.300
capable of generating longer videos of&nbsp;
about 2 minutes [not a diffusion model].

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:34.800
The videos of Phenaki are kind of creepy&nbsp;
and not that realistic, but they are long!

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.040
So today, we are diving in into video diffusion&nbsp;
models and explain how they work and in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.800
way Make-a-Video from MetaAI and Imagen Video&nbsp;
from Google differ and what they have in common.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:50.580
If you are not yet familiar with how&nbsp;
diffusion models generate images,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.900
then do check out our previous videos on&nbsp;
this first. It is important to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.900 --> 00:05:00.000
the underlying image mechanisms if we&nbsp;
are to comprehend video diffusion models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:04.620
because you can think of images&nbsp;
being videos with a single frame.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.900
For this explanation, we will assume that we&nbsp;
already know what an image diffusion model is.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:15.420
We start our explanations of text-to-video&nbsp;
generators by explaining Make-A-Video from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:21.000
Meta AI. We choose this one for the&nbsp;
start, because it is a special kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:25.980
of video diffusion model: it is a natural&nbsp;
enhancement of text-to-image generation,&nbsp;

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:32.640
because it uses text-to-image diffusion models&nbsp;
to build them in as-are for video diffusion.

00:05:32.640 --> 00:05:38.100
And since we assume we are already&nbsp;
familiarized with image diffusion models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.320
Make-A-Video is a great place to start.

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:46.320
How to generate a short video with&nbsp;
diffusion models? The core idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.300
Make-A-Video is to take a text-to-image&nbsp;
diffusion model that already knows how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.680
visual world looks like and what&nbsp;
its correspondence with text is.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:01.080
Then, it extends the text-to-image model&nbsp;
with video processing schnick-schnack and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:07.200
uses video data (unlabeled, so unpaired)&nbsp;
to learn realistic motion. In this way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:12.240
one does not need paired text-video data, which&nbsp;
makes the training data requirements so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:17.640
better. This is a way to generate short,&nbsp;
low resolution and low framerate models.

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:21.960
Then additional models can take care of&nbsp;
interpolating between frames to increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.400
the frame rate, then super-resolution&nbsp;
models can take care of transforming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.360
frames into high resolution.
Let’s take it step by step.

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:36.780
First, we have the text-to-image diffusion model,&nbsp;
which shares the core components with DALL-E 2.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:43.440
The text-to-image diffusion model takes in noise&nbsp;
and step by step, it generates images of 64x64&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:50.400
resolution. It considers the text information in&nbsp;
the following way: The CLIP text encoder embeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:56.580
the text into a multimodal visual-language&nbsp;
space. A so-called “prior” network translates&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:02.580
the text vector into the corresponding&nbsp;
image vector in the CLIP embedding space.

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:08.040
Because we remember that CLIP was&nbsp;
trained to embed images and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:15.060
captions into vectors close to each other. Then&nbsp;
the diffusion model takes in this image vector,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:20.820
which is a visual equivalent of the&nbsp;
text prompt and uses it to gradually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:28.500
generate a faithful image to the text prompt.
Ok, so this was the DALL-E 2 recap in a nutshell.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.880
This text-to-image diffusion model,&nbsp;
trained on paired text-image data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:37.560
(so usually images and their captions)&nbsp;
can now generate pictures from text.

00:07:37.560 --> 00:07:40.860
A first super-resolution&nbsp;
network enhances the image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:49.740
to 256x256 resolution and a second one&nbsp;
to 768x768 pixels. Much like DALL-E 2.

00:07:49.740 --> 00:07:54.600
Now we are ready for the video-specific&nbsp;
technical schnick-schnack. The U-Net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.600 --> 00:08:00.000
producing clearer and clearer images at each&nbsp;
diffusion step so far, had two-dimensional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.640
convolution and attention layers to ensure&nbsp;
coherence in the 2d structure of the image.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:12.300
Now for video, the U-Net needs to be extended&nbsp;
into the temporal dimension to ensure temporal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:19.020
coherence. For the convolution layers, the authors&nbsp;
could have used 3D convolutions, but that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:24.300
have been computationally expensive, so instead&nbsp;
they do the image-like 2D convolution as before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:30.480
and add a 1D convolution following it, to&nbsp;
combine the frames, so the temporal dimension.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:37.140
This means, the network can keep all its trained&nbsp;
image convolution weights and only the 1D,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:41.100
temporal convolution layers need&nbsp;
to be trained from scratch. Cool.

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:46.800
To use the authors words, at initialization,&nbsp;
because the 1D convolutions connecting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:51.960
temporal dimension are random, the network&nbsp;
will generate different images at each frame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.960 --> 00:08:55.440
each describing the text, but&nbsp;
lacking temporal coherence.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:59.040
For the attention layers, the authors&nbsp;
do a similar thing to the convolution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:05.160
especially since 3D attention would have been even&nbsp;
more expensive than 3D convolutions. Following&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:09.840
each spatial attention layer, which is already&nbsp;
there from the pretrained text-to-image U-Net,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.840 --> 00:09:15.300
they stack an attention layer after the spatial&nbsp;
attention, to combine the temporal dimension.

00:09:15.300 --> 00:09:21.120
Now, you can imagine why this approach only&nbsp;
generated short videos: as the videos grow longer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.160
so does the number of frames one needs to&nbsp;
generate, so the attention window grows too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:30.720
so computation scales quadratically.&nbsp;
I wonder if windowed attention like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.380
in the Swin Transformer would help&nbsp;
diminish the compute issues here.

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:38.940
To make the generated videos have&nbsp;
a higher frame rate, the authors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.940 --> 00:09:44.820
use a frame interpolation network trained on&nbsp;
general video data to predict masked frames.

00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:51.780
So how is this whole thing trained? Many things&nbsp;
are trained independently. The authors train the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:56.760
“prior” network and the super-resolution&nbsp;
networks on images alone. They train the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:01.620
text-to-image diffusion model on paired&nbsp;
text-image data, no fine-tuning on videos!

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:06.660
Then the authors add the video-specific&nbsp;
convolution and attention layers and train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.660 --> 00:10:13.020
on unlabeled video data. All in all, this work&nbsp;
is a beast of a network that makes clever use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:18.600
of existing models and of easily available&nbsp;
data: It is easier to find images and their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:24.180
captions than visual descriptions of videos (not&nbsp;
subtitles!) on a large scale on the internet.

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:30.180
Let’s summarize Make-A-Video to later&nbsp;
compare it to Imagen Video. We have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.180 --> 00:10:35.160
input text prompt and run it through an&nbsp;
encoder to transform it into a vector.

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:41.400
A prior network translates the CLIP text&nbsp;
vector into the corresponding CLIP image&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:47.340
vector. Then a Base diffusion model which was&nbsp;
first trained to generate images from text,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:51.840
just like DALL-E 2, gets additional&nbsp;
convolution and attention layers onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:55.800
the temporal dimension to combine different&nbsp;
frames, so single images, into a video.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:01.380
A temporal super-resolution network does&nbsp;
frame interpolation, so it increases the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:06.240
temporal resolution by producing more&nbsp;
and more frames in between frames.

00:11:06.240 --> 00:11:09.660
Then two spatial-super resolution models upscale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:13.920
the individual frames to increase the&nbsp;
resolution of the video, frame by frame.

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:17.460
Now we know how Make-A-Video works. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:22.320
let’s look at Google’s approach: How does&nbsp;
Imagen Video generate videos with diffusion?

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:29.700
To put it this way, what would we need to change&nbsp;
in Make-A-Video to make Imagen Video? We would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.700 --> 00:11:35.880
need to change this cascade of diffusion models&nbsp;
to end up with a cascade of 7 models. First,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.880 --> 00:11:41.820
we would need to replace the text encoder&nbsp;
by a frozen T5-XXL text transformer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.240
which was trained on lots&nbsp;
and lots of text-only data.

00:11:45.240 --> 00:11:51.180
Then, a little different is how the base video&nbsp;
diffusion model is trained. Architecturally, it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.180 --> 00:11:56.760
is also a U-Net enhanced with temporal attention&nbsp;
and temporal convolutions to bind the individual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:03.480
images together which are part of the same video.&nbsp;
It is basically the training of this that differs.

00:12:05.940 --> 00:12:11.400
The authors train it on images and videos&nbsp;
simultaneously, where they treat single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:16.020
images as single frame videos. But this&nbsp;
means that for the video training data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.020 --> 00:12:21.480
they do need aligned video and text description&nbsp;
(which means they use a Google-internal dataset&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:26.220
for this), while Make-A-Video got&nbsp;
away without aligned text-video data.

00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:30.180
The story continues like for Imagen,&nbsp;
with a temporal supersampling network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.180 --> 00:12:35.460
that produces more frames. It is followed&nbsp;
by two spatial-super resolution models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:40.020
like with Make-A-Video. But then we have&nbsp;
two more temporal upsampling models to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.020 --> 00:12:45.540
produce more frames, then yet another&nbsp;
spatial supersampling. Done. This,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:50.040
in a nutshell, is the raw difference&nbsp;
between Imagen Video and Make-A-Video.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:53.880
And with classifier-free guidance,&nbsp;
which we covered in another video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.880 --> 00:12:58.800
and with some other little tricks, this&nbsp;
cascade of 7 models just seems to work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:05.340
which must be a great achievement for the&nbsp;
authors, since this is direct follow-up of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:10.140
their previous work on diffusion models marking&nbsp;
the territory in video diffusion modelling.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:13.920
In their previous work, they were proving&nbsp;
the concept that video diffusion models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.920 --> 00:13:22.200
are possible by producing 64-frames&nbsp;
of 128x128 at 24 frames per second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:30.000
Now Imagen can do 128 frames long&nbsp;
1280x768 HD videos, also at 24 FPS.

00:13:30.000 --> 00:13:34.320
The multitude of ways to do and&nbsp;
to combine things in the diffusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.320 --> 00:13:38.100
model space and the lack of insight
for why a thing works and in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.100 --> 00:13:42.840
constellation it doesn’t, makes it so hard&nbsp;
to wrap Ms. Coffee Bean’s head around this.

00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:47.220
Her hypothesis is that nobody knows&nbsp;
why diffusion models work and which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:51.840
combination of tricks is the best and why&nbsp;
tricks do the trick in the first place.

00:13:52.620 --> 00:13:59.100
If you are interested in even more video diffusion&nbsp;
models, do check out Phenaki, which is also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:59.100 --> 00:14:04.320
video diffusion model published the same day&nbsp;
as Imagen Video from another group at Google,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:11.040
capable of producing long video, where of course,&nbsp;
the realism suffers a bit at this kind of length.

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.820
Ms. Coffee Bean cannot wait&nbsp;
to see what kind of long and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:19.800
realistic videos will come out of the&nbsp;
diffusion models of the next 3 months.

00:14:19.800 --> 00:14:24.660
What do you think of this diffusion model&nbsp;
flurry? How are you going to use them in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:30.060
your work and everyday life? We hope to read from&nbsp;
you in the comments. See you in the next video!

00:14:30.060 --> 00:14:31.680
Okay, bye!

